                  snippet:
                    "I would like to have a quick chat about it next week at [desired location]. Would either [two choices of date and time] work for you? ... Keep it casual so it truly sounds like you can help instead of coming off as trying to sell them something ...\nSubject Line: Requesting to schedule a meeting at your earliest convenience · Dear [Mr./Ms. Last Name], I hope this email finds you well.\nI would like to have a quick chat about it next week at [desired location]. Would either [two choices of date and time] work for you? ... Keep it casual so it truly sounds like you can help instead of coming off as trying to sell them something they may not need. If your reputation speaks for itself, let it be your main selling point with this sample email: Subject Line: Thank you for scheduling a meeting with our CEO\nBefore UpLead, he led customer acquisition for Dropbox, Morgan Stanley, ADP, and dozens of Fortune 500 companies at BWG Marketing. He has sent over 100 million cold emails and built growth systems used by thousands of sales teams worldwide. ... In this article, you’ll find everything you need to start writing meeting request emails that get results.\nI agree to receive emails from UpLead, and can unsubscribe at any time. A meeting request email is a written message asking for an appointment with someone.",
