                  snippet:
                    "Here’s how to ask for availability for a meeting without much back-and-forth. I’ve also included a professional email template and some examples you can copy.\nIt gives the other person context right away and makes the request feel more purposeful. For example, instead of saying, “Can we meet sometime next week?” say, “I’d like to meet to review the Q3 campaign results on Zoom and align on next steps.” · A little more detail can help the other person decide how urgent or useful the meeting is.\nShe publishes weekly educational videos and articles on building AI assistants – And yes, she’s a real person! ... Jack is GM of New Verticals at Lindy, where he’s focused on exploring how AI agents can be applied to new industries and niche problems alike. ... Knowing how to ask for availability for a meeting can save you a lot of back-and-forth and help you get replies faster. After analyzing hundreds of meeting requests, I break down the exact format to use, common mistakes to avoid, and examples you can copy.\nSkipping the meeting length: Let people know whether you need 15, 20, or 30 minutes. Sounding too demanding: Keep the tone polite and give them room to suggest another time. Ignoring time zones: Always double-check the timing when the other person works in a different location. Using only a scheduling link in cold outreach: A link can help, but it should not replace a thoughtful request. ... Lindy is an AI assistant you can text to help with email, scheduling, reminders, and calendar management.\nDraft meeting request emails: Ask Lindy to write a clear, professional email based on the person, topic, and tone you want.",
