                  title:
                    "(PDF) The Impact of Remote Work on Employee Productivity and Satisfaction: Analyze How Remote Work Trends Have Affected Various Aspects of Employee Performance And Well-Being",
                  snippet:
                    "Some studies report considerable improvements in employee performance and satisfaction (Fathima and Suresh Kumar, 2025) while others emphasise contextual factors such as organisational culture, digital capability and employee adaptability (Maity ...\nSome studies report considerable improvements in employee performance and satisfaction (Fathima and Suresh Kumar, 2025) while others emphasise contextual factors such as organisational culture, digital capability and employee adaptability (Maity and Lee, 2025). These inconsistencies suggest that more underlying behavioural and technological processes define productivity outcomes rather than remote work policies.\nAlthough scholarly interest has increased, there has been little research that integrates technology acceptance, task-technology fit, and knowledge-based perspectives into a single framework to explain how technology-driven behavioural intentions can improve productivity in remote work. Most previous papers investigate organisational-level adoption outcomes (Ferdous et al., 2025) or the impact of digital intention on employee satisfaction and performance (Sansovini, 2025), leaving a gap in the literature on how to directly link digital intention mechanisms to quantifiable productivity outcomes.\n(Fathima & Suresh Kumar, 2025). A pilot test with 30 respondents was conducted to ensure clarity, reliability, and construct validity. ... ... First, it reconceptualises remote work productivity as a behaviourally mediated technological outcome rather than a direct consequence of remote policy implementation.\n(Fathima & Suresh Kumar, 2025), this study positions behavioural intention as the central explanatory mechanism linking digital perceptions and productivity outcomes. Second, integrating Technology Acceptance, Task-Technology Fit and Knowledge-Based perspectives extends existing remote work theory beyond isolated frameworks.",
