                  filename: null,
                  title: "Fiscal Quarter: What It Is, How It’s Used, and More",
                  snippet:
                    "In this example, the phrase Q4 2025 means the fourth quarter of 2025. Because this phrase includes both the quarter and the year, this phrase assumes that whatever is being analyzed has a fiscal year that is the same as a calendar year.\nIn this example, the phrase Q4 2025 means the fourth quarter of 2025. Because this phrase includes both the quarter and the year, this phrase assumes that whatever is being analyzed has a fiscal year that is the same as a calendar year. A fiscal calendar is an arbitrary range of dates that defines a company’s annual reporting cycle.\nFor instance, if a company chooses to have its fiscal year starting in February rather than January, then its first quarter would consist of February, March, and April. Companies sometimes choose to do this if they want their fiscal year to end in their own peak season. Alternatively, since finishing the year often involves a lot of additional accounting work, some companies choose to end their fiscal year on a relatively calm month. There are two components to Q4 2025.\nA fiscal quarter is a three-month period on a company’s financial calendar that acts as a basis for reporting earnings and paying dividends.\nAs its name suggests, there are four quarterly periods in a year, meaning a publicly traded company would issue four quarterly reports per year. Companies and investors alike use fiscal quarters to keep track of their financial results and business developments over time. These quarters are often referred to as Q1, Q2, Q3, and Q4.",
